prompt:
Use Three.js to implement a toy car maze navigation game.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Strictly Followed)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { GLTFLoader } from 'three/addons/loaders/GLTFLoader.js';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[Model Loading]
(1) Use GLTFLoader to load assets/ToyCar.glb;
(2) Place the car at the maze starting point (top-left corner);

[Maze Generation]
(3) Generate an 8x8 maze (using recursive division or a DFS algorithm);
(4) Use thin rectangular boxes for the walls (BoxGeometry, gray);
(5) Use a light gray plane for the ground;
(6) Mark the finish point (bottom-right corner) with a green square;

[Driving Controls]
(7) Arrow key controls: ↑ accelerate, ↓ brake, ←→ steer;
(8) Acceleration: 3 m/s²; maximum speed: 8 m/s;
(9) Inertia decay coefficient: 0.95;

[Collision]
(10) Use AABB collision detection between the car and the walls, and bounce the car back upon collision;

[Collectibles]
(11) Place 3 small yellow stars in the maze (SphereGeometry);
(12) Collect a star when the car passes it (distance < 0.5);

[Camera]
(13) Use a fixed top-down camera (orthographic or perspective, looking downward along the y-axis);

[HUD Display]
(14) HUD in the top-left corner:
  - Timer (id="time")
  - Number of stars collected (id="stars")
  - Speed (id="speed")
  - Position (id="position")

[State Exposure (Required)]
Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame:
  - modelLoaded: boolean
  - time: number (seconds)
  - starsCollected: number (0-3)
  - speed: number
  - position: {x,z}
  - completed: boolean

Output a single standalone HTML file.
